==Old English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|ang|ih}}

===Etymology===
From {{proto|Germanic|ek||ik|lang=ang}}, from {{proto|Indo-European|éǵh₂|lang=ang}}. Germanic cognates include Old Frisian {{term|ik|lang=ofs}}, Old Saxon {{term|ik|lang=osx}}, Old Dutch {{term|ik|lang=odt}} (Dutch {{term|ik|lang=nl}}), Old High German {{term|ih|lang=goh}} (German {{term|ich|lang=de}}), Old Norse {{term|ek|lang=non}} (Swedish {{term|jag|lang=sv}}), Gothic {{term|𐌹𐌺|tr=ik|lang=got}}. The Indo-European root, in various forms, is also the source of Sanskrit {{term|अहम्|tr=ahám|lang=sa}}, Latin {{term|ego|egō|lang=la}} (French {{term|je|lang=fr}}, Spanish {{term|yo|lang=es}}, Italian {{term|io|lang=it}} etc.), Ancient Greek {{term|ἐγώ|lang=grc}}, Lithuanian {{term|aš|lang=lt}}, Latvian {{term|es|lang=lv}}, Avestan ''azəm''{{rfscript|Avst}}, Old Church Slavonic {{term|азъ|tr=azŭ|lang=cu}} (Russian {{term|я|lang=ru}}), Old Armenian {{term|ես|tr=es|lang=xcl}}. For declined derivations, see under {{term|me|mē|lang=ang}}, {{term|we|wē|lang=ang}} etc.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/itʃ/|/ik/|lang=ang}}

===Pronoun===
{{head|ang|personal pronoun|head=iċ}}

# [[I]], used by the speaker referring to himself or herself as the subject, or in agreement with that subject

